  {
   "id": "mtdb8thgrlo6l",
   "chapterId": "mtdb8the4ti2p",
   "title": "Lesson goals",
   "body": "By the end of Lesson 1 you should be able to:\n\n  • greet people at different times of the day        (unsure? p. 10)\n  • address someone formally and informally           (unsure? p. 12)\n  • introduce yourself and say your name              (unsure? p. 13)\n  • ask other people's names and how they're doing    (unsure? p. 16)\n  • put it all together in a simple conversation      (unsure? p. 19)\n\nAbbreviations used throughout the course:\n  (m.) masculine · (f.) feminine · (sg.) singular · (pl.) plural\n  (fml.) formal · (infml.) informal · (lit.) literally\n\nAudio: 1A Vocabulary Builder 1 = CD 1 track 2 · 1B Take It Further = track 3\n1C Grammar Builder 1 = track 4 · 1D Vocabulary Builder 2 = track 5\n1E Take It Further = track 6 · 1F Grammar Builder 2 = track 7\n1G Bring It All Together = track 8 · 1H Take It Further = track 9",
   "source": "p. 9",
   "date": "2026-08-28"
  },
  {
   "id": "mtdb8thgask53",
   "chapterId": "mtdb8the4ti2p",
   "title": "Pronunciation — accents and special symbols",
   "body": "~ til (tilde) — produces a nasal vowel\n    não (no)          like the ou in bound\n    aviões (airplanes) like the o in don't, followed by the e in get\n    mãe (mother)      like the u in thunder, followed by the e in get\n\n´ agudo (acute) — stress falls on that syllable; on e and o it also marks an open vowel\n    café (coffee)     like the e in best\n    nós (we)          like the o in off (northeastern U.S. pronunciation)\n    possível (possible) like the i in routine\n\n^ circunflexo (circumflex) — the vowel is stressed and closed\n    você (you)        like the ay in day, but clipped\n    avô (grandfather) like the o in go, but clipped\n\n` grave (grave) — distinguishes grammatical category only; no change in sound\n    a (the) / à (to the)\n\n¸ cedilha (cedilla) — on c before a, o, u to give it an s sound\n    açúcar (sugar)    sounds like the s in see\n                      (note the second c is pronounced like the k in kite)",
   "source": "pp. 11–12 (1B Take It Further)",
   "date": "2026-08-28"
  },
  {
   "id": "mtdb8thgsu7vu",
   "chapterId": "mtdb8the4ti2p",
   "title": "Pronunciation — M and N",
   "body": "M and N at the end of a word, or in the middle before a consonant, nasalize the\nvowel before them and aren't really pronounced on their own.\n\n  ponte (bridge)   the n makes the o nasal, like the on in don't\n  cama (bed)       the first a is nasal like the un in bunch;\n                   the second a is NOT nasal — it sounds like the a in father\n  bom (good)       a word-final m is pronounced as an n; the om sounds like on in don't\n\nCareful: an m or n BETWEEN two vowels forms a syllable with the second vowel, so the\nfirst vowel is not nasalized. Compare — first word nasal, second not:\n\n  limpa (clean)  /  lima (lime)\n  grande (big)   /  grana (money, infml.)\n\nAt the START of a word, m and n sound like English:\n  mala (suitcase) — m as in Mary        nada (nothing) — n as in nest",
   "source": "pp. 15–16 (1E Take It Further)",
   "date": "2026-08-28"
  },
  {
   "id": "mtdb8thgrm8fy",
   "chapterId": "mtdb8the4ti2p",
   "title": "Greetings — formal and informal",
   "body": "GENDER ON OBRIGADO/A\nobrigado/a (thank you) has two endings because Portuguese nouns and adjectives carry\ngender. You say what YOU are: a woman says obrigada, a man says obrigado.\n\nWHO YOU'RE TALKING TO\nvocê (you) works for men and women alike and is the informal default — family, friends.\nTo be formal, swap você for o senhor (sir) or a senhora (ma'am), matching the gender of\nthe person you're addressing.\n\n  How are you?      Como vai você? (infml.) / Como vai o senhor? (fml./m.)\n                    / Como vai a senhora? (fml./f.)\n  I'm fine, and you? Tudo bem, e você? / Estou bem, e o senhor? / Estou bem, e a senhora?\n\nSENHOR / SENHORA WITH NAMES\nBefore a last name, senhor / senhora works like Mr. / Mrs.–Ms., and reads as the highest\nlevel of formality in Brazil. Written abbreviations: Sr. (Mr.), Sra. (Mrs./Ms.).\nWith a FIRST name it's also common — Senhor shortens to Seu (Seu Agenor), and Senhora\ncan become Dona (Dona Maria).\n\n  Informal:     Você fala português, Paulo?\n  Formal:       O senhor fala português, Sr. Paulo?\n  Very formal:  O senhor fala português, Sr. Smith?\n\nTWO PAIRS WORTH NOT MIXING UP\n  Você pode me ajudar? (Can you help me?) — asking a favor\n  Socorro! (Help!)                        — an emergency\n\n  Faça-me um favor. (Do me a favor.)      — asking a favor\n  Faça o favor.                           — informal softener on a request or command\n                                            e.g. Abra a janela, faça o favor.\n                                            (Open the window, will you?)",
   "source": "pp. 12–13, 16–17 (Grammar Builders 1 & 2)",
   "date": "2026-08-28"
  },
  {
   "id": "mtdb8thgd0yik",
   "chapterId": "mtdb8the4ti2p",
   "title": "The dialogue — Sandra, Gustavo and Jane",
   "body": "An informal three-way introduction. Sandra runs into Gustavo, greets him with\nOi and E aí?, and he answers Tudo bem. She introduces Jane with Esta é a Jane; Gustavo\nand Jane exchange Prazer. Gustavo asks Você é americana? — Jane answers Não, eu sou\naustraliana — and he welcomes her with Bem-vinda ao Brasil! Jane closes with Legal!\n\nStructures to notice:\n  • Esta é … to introduce a third person\n  • Prazer / Muito prazer as a two-way exchange on being introduced\n  • adjectives of nationality agree with the speaker: americana, australiana (f.)\n  • bem-vindo / bem-vinda agrees with the person being welcomed\n\nWork Out 2 (p. 20) rewrites this same dialogue formally, using Sra. Dias, Sr. Morais and\nSr. Smith: Como vai o senhor? / Estou bem / Quero lhe apresentar / Muito prazer /\nO prazer é meu / O senhor é americano? / Não / Bem-vindo / Muito obrigado.",
   "source": "p. 19 (1G Bring It All Together, CD 1 track 8)",
   "date": "2026-08-28"
  },
  {
   "id": "mtdb8thgtmaj3",
   "chapterId": "mtdb8the4ti2p",
   "title": "Production practice — Drive It Home",
   "body": "Say these out loud, then check yourself.\n\n1. Greet a friend in the morning and ask if everything is fine.\n   → Bom dia, tudo bem?\n2. Greet an elderly neighbour in the evening and ask how she is.\n   → Boa noite, como vai a senhora?\n3. Introduce Rita, a colleague, to Mr. Silva, and tell her Mr. Silva is Brazilian.\n   → Sr. Silva, quero lhe apresentar Rita. Rita, Sr. Silva é brasileiro.\n4. Introduce Antônio, a friend, to another friend, Sandra.\n   → Antônio, gostaria de lhe apresentar Sandra.\n5. Greet an elderly woman in the afternoon and ask her name.\n   → Boa tarde. Como a senhora se chama?\n6. Say thank you and good-bye to a salesman at a shop.\n   → Muito obrigado / muito obrigada. Até logo.\n7. Apologize, say you didn't understand, then ask a gentleman to repeat.\n   → Desculpe, eu não entendi. O senhor poderia repetir?\n8. Say excuse me, ask a stranger if she speaks English, then say you don't speak\n   Portuguese.\n   → Com licença, a senhora fala inglês? Eu não falo português.\n\nWORD RECALL (p. 23) — how would you answer?\n1. Obrigada. → De nada.\n2. Você é brasileiro? → Não, eu sou …\n3. Como vai? → Tudo bem.\n4. Até logo. → Até logo.\n5. Você fala português? → Sim, eu falo um pouco de português. / Sim, estou aprendendo\n   português.\n6. Gostaria de lhe apresentar a Sra. Medeiros. → Muito prazer, Sra. Medeiros.\n7. Como você se chama? → Eu me chamo …\n8. Parabéns! → Obrigado / obrigada.",
   "source": "p. 21",
   "date": "2026-08-28"
  }
